 "title": "JWT vs session",
 "sources": [
  "OWASP Session Management Cheat Sheet (fetched 2026-11-17): a session ID is assigned at session creation; the meaning and business logic associated with the session ID must be stored on the server side; the app must invalidate the session at least on the server side on logout; deleting a cookie alone does not invalidate a stolen copy of the session ID. https://cheatsheetseries.owasp.org/cheatsheets/Session_Management_Cheat_Sheet.html",
  "RFC 7519, JSON Web Token (fetched 2026-11-17): a JWT is a compact, URL-safe means of representing claims; it is a sequence of base64url parts separated by periods (header, claims set, signature in the signed form); the optional 'exp' claim is the time on or after which the JWT MUST NOT be accepted; the RFC says nothing about revoking a token before exp. https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc7519",
  "Engineering judgment, not from the sources: signature-only verification needs no server lookup; short-lived access tokens limit the damage of a stolen one."
 ],
 "lines": [
  {
   "text": "After you log in, the server has to remember who you are, and there are two common ways to do that.",
   "caps": "*LOGGED IN | SERVER MUST *REMEMBER YOU | *TWO WAYS"
  },
  {
   "text": "With a session cookie, the server stores your session, and your browser just carries an ID.",
   "caps": "SESSION *COOKIE | SERVER *STORES IT | BROWSER CARRIES *AN ID"
  },
  {
   "text": "The ID alone means nothing, since the real data stays on the server, which can delete it any time.",
   "caps": "ID *MEANS NOTHING | DATA STAYS *ON SERVER | DELETE *ANY TIME"
  },
  {
   "text": "With a JWT, you carry the proof yourself: a signed token with a header, the claims, and a signature.",
   "caps": "*JWT | YOU CARRY *THE PROOF | HEADER, *CLAIMS, SIGNATURE"
  },
  {
   "text": "The server just checks the signature, so it doesn't have to look up a session at all.",
   "caps": "CHECK THE *SIGNATURE | NO *LOOKUP"
  },
  {
   "text": "But here's the catch: the JWT spec has an expiry claim called exp, but says nothing about revoking a token early.",
   "caps": "BUT THERE IS A *CATCH | HAS *EXPIRY | NO *REVOKE"
  },
  {
   "text": "So a stolen token keeps working until it expires, which is why short expiry times matter.",
   "caps": "STOLEN TOKEN *STILL WORKS | UNTIL *EXPIRY | KEEP IT *SHORT"
  },
  {
   "text": "So if you need instant logout, use server sessions, or keep JWTs short and plan for revocation.",
   "caps": "NEED *INSTANT LOGOUT? | USE *SESSIONS | OR *SHORT JWTS"
  }
 ],
